== Glossary

Terms are grouped by area. Definitions describe the vanilla game as played in Wave 0; Wave 1–2 mods may adjust specifics — the mod cards in Part 2 are the authority where they do.

=== Hunting Basics

*Hunter Rank (HR):* The base game's progression rank, earned through Hub quests. Rising HR unlocks quests, monsters, and gear tiers.

*Master Rank (MR):* Sunbreak's progression tier above HR — harder monsters, MR-only gear, and the expansion's systems.

*Quest:* A self-contained hunt or gathering task with a timer, a fail condition (usually three faints), and rewards.

*Urgent quest:* A story-gating quest that unlocks the next star tier when cleared.

*Subquest:* An optional side checklist running alongside your quest — clear it for bonus rewards.

*Village quests:* Single-player story quests set in Kamura; the base game's tutorial-campaign.

*Hub quests:* Multiplayer-scaled quests from the Gathering Hub; where HR progression lives.

*Event quests:* Special quests with unique rewards, distributed via the game's event quest system.

*Faint / Cart:* To die in a quest and be carried back to camp. Three carts fail the quest.

*Carve:* To harvest materials from a slain monster's body (usually three carves).

*Capture:* Ending a hunt with a trap + tranq bombs on a weakened monster — faster and often more rewarding than a kill.

*Trap (shock / pitfall):* An item that immobilizes a monster briefly — required for capture, useful for free damage.

*Tranq bomb:* The bomb used to put a trapped, weakened monster to sleep for capture.

*Part break:* Destroying a monster's breakable part (head, tail, claws) — staggers it and yields extra rewards.

*Tail sever:* Cutting off a monster's tail with cutting damage — removes tail attacks and grants a carve.

*Hit zone:* A body part's damage modifier. Weak spots (often the head) take noticeably more damage.

*Limping:* The weakened walking state of a near-death monster — your cue to finish or capture.

*Skull icon:* The minimap indicator that a monster is capturable — appears once your tracking level is high enough.

=== Combat

*Wirebug:* Rise's grappling insect — the tool for leaps, wirefalls, and silkbind attacks.

*Wirefall:* Pressing the wirebug action while knocked down to recover instantly.

*Silkbind attack:* A weapon's special wirebug move, on a cooldown.

*Switch Skill:* An alternate move for a weapon slot, unlocked through quests and requests.

*Switch Skill Swap (Scroll):* Sunbreak's two-loadout system — swap between Red and Blue scrolls mid-hunt.

*Great Wirebug:* A fixed field point that launches you across the map.

*Wyvern ride:* The mounted state where you briefly control a monster and slam it into walls or other monsters.

*Mounting damage:* The hidden meter built by aerial/wirebug attacks that triggers the wyvern ride.

*Blight:* A debuff applied by elemental attacks (fire, water, thunder, ice) — cured with nullberries.

*Status (poison / paralysis / sleep / blast):* Monster-applicable effects with buildup meters; each proc delivers its effect.

*Stun:* The knocked-out state from repeated blunt head hits — you are defenseless while stunned.

*Affinity:* Critical hit chance; critical hits deal extra damage.

*Sharpness:* Melee weapon durability, restored with a whetstone. Higher tiers (green, blue, white) deal more damage.

*Element:* A weapon's elemental damage type (fire, water, thunder, ice, dragon) — matched against monster weaknesses.

*Dango:* The canteen meal — stat boosts plus a food skill before every hunt.

=== Gear & Progression

*Smithy:* The village workshop where weapons, armor, decorations, and buddy gear are forged and upgraded.

*Forge / Upgrade:* Create a new weapon or advance one along its upgrade tree.

*Armor skill:* Passive effects granted by armor skill points; they activate at thresholds.

*Decoration:* A craftable gem socketed into armor's decoration slots to add skills.

*Talisman:* An equipable charm with random skills, obtained through melding.

*Melding:* Trading materials at the smithy for random talismans.

*Armor sphere:* The upgrade material that raises armor defense.

*Layered armor:* Cosmetic armor that changes appearance without changing stats.

*Layered weapon:* Sunbreak's cosmetic weapon system — any look on any weapon.

*Zenny:* The game's money, from quests and selling parts.

*Kamura Points:* A second currency from gathering and hunting, spent at facilities.

=== Monsters & Locations

*Rampage:* The base game's siege defense of Kamura — waves of monsters vs. your walls and weapons.

*Afflicted monster:* A qurio-cursed monster with glowing clusters that must be broken; drops afflicted materials.

*Anomaly Investigation:* Sunbreak's endgame quest system — leveled investigations against afflicted monsters.

*Risen elder:* The apex endgame monsters — risen versions of elder dragons, fought late in the ladder.

*Elder Dragon:* The most powerful monster class — tough fights, strong gear.

*Elgado:* Sunbreak's hub, home of Master Rank quests and followers.

*Kamura Village:* The base game's home village.

*Subcamp:* A campsite in the field that acts as a fast-travel point.

*Spiribird:* Colored endemic birds that grant temporary stat buffs when collected.

*Endemic life:* The collectible creatures and hazards of the field — buffs, lures, and environmental tools.

*Hunter's Notes:* The in-game encyclopedia of monsters, weaknesses, materials, and quests.

*Tracking level:* The map visibility meter built by collecting monster tracks.

=== Buddies

*Palamute:* Your riding dog companion — fast travel and combat support.

*Palico:* Your support cat companion, with a role defining its behavior (healer, trapper, assist, and more).

*Meowcenaries:* Buddy expeditions that gather materials on a timer.

*Argosy:* The trading ship that brings materials or points on schedule.

*Cohoot:* Your owl scout — check its nest for daily free items.

=== Sunbreak Systems

*Follower:* An NPC hunter who joins your quests via Follower Quests — capable AI partners for solo play.

*Follower Quest:* The quests that unlock and level followers.

*Qurio:* The crimson curse driving monsters into afflicted states — the expansion's plot and endgame driver.

*Qurio Crafting:* Weapon augment crafting — spend afflicted materials to roll bonuses (attack, element, slots, skills) onto weapons.

*Augment:* A Qurio Crafting bonus rolled onto a weapon.

*Special Investigation / Hazard quest:* The deliberately overtuned endgame quests — optional mastery goals.

=== Modding & Tools

*Mod:* A user-made change installed into the game — this list's subject matter (owner-supplied).

*Mod card:* The entry format used in Part 2: name + URL, dependencies, impact, notes.

*Mod manager:* Software that installs/toggles mods without manual file editing — this project uses Fluffy Manager 5000.

*Fluffy Manager 5000:* The mod organizer for this list — see Part 1, Setup.

*nativePC:* The game-folder overlay directory where Fluffy Manager 5000 installs mod files.

*Dependency:* A framework or mod another mod requires — install dependencies first.

*REFramework:* The common script framework many MHR mods depend on — a user-supplied mod in this list when a card lists it.

*Load order:* The install order of mods; for overlapping files, the lower/later entry wins.

*Purge mods:* The manager's "reset to vanilla" action — uninstalls everything it installed.
